{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":1004,"items":[{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,19]],"date-time":"2026-10-19T19:43:16Z","timestamp":1792438996004,"version":"4.5.0"},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"American Society of Mechanical Engineers","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,15]],"date-time":"2026-06-15T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1781481600000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/www.asme.org\/publications-submissions\/publishing-information\/legal-policies"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["asmedigitalcollection.asme.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,15]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Accurate measurement of surface temperature on aero-engine hot-section components is critical for development and performance validation. Conventional infrared thermometry, however, suffers from interference by combustion gases (CO2, H2O) and unknown, dynamic surface emissivity. This work presents a visible-near-infrared multispectral radiometric method to mitigate gas interference. A hybrid penalty-function algorithm simultaneously retrieves temperature and spectral emissivity without prior emissivity modeling. A two-dimensional (2D) multispectral endoscopic imaging system is developed for spatially resolved measurement. Experiments were conducted on typical engine alloys (DD6, DZ125, GH3030) heated in a methane-air combustion environment using four selected bands (650\u2013850 nm). Results show the system successfully reconstructs 2D temperature fields, with deviations within 2.2% compared to thermocouple readings, confirming accuracy and robustness for 2D temperature measurements in hot-gas-filled environments.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1115\/gt2026-175466","type":"proceedings-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,19]],"date-time":"2026-10-19T18:55:42Z","timestamp":1792436142000},"update-policy":"h